 */

if ( ! defined( 'ABSPATH' ) ) {
	exit;
}

return array(
	'stay_word' => 'Stay',
	'stay_intro' => 'Akari Hotel runs the day in four hours, from check-in at 15:00 to breakfast the next morning. The house is a 1931 printing works that opened as a hotel in 2019, at 18 Tessel Street in Wrenfield.',
	'stay_cta' => 'See the rooms',
	'stay_wide_label' => '( From 15:00 )',
	'stay_1_h' => '15:00 Arrival',
	'stay_1_p1' => 'Check-in opens at 15:00. At the oak desk, Tomás Ferreira and the front office team match your name to the room, hand over a brass key, and point you toward the lift. The front desk stays open twenty-four hours, so a later train is only a matter of telling us. Wrenfield Central station is a six-minute walk from 18 Tessel Street.',
	'stay_1_p2' => 'If you reach the door before the room is free, leave the suitcase in the lobby and sit with a book; Lucie Morel’s housekeeping team sends the bag up once the room is ready. The house was a printing works in 1931 and opened as Akari Hotel in 2019. Forty-two rooms sit above this floor: twenty-four Studios, twelve Corner rooms, and six Lofts. Arrival is unhurried. There is no queue to perform, and no one asks you to stand at a podium.',
	'stay_2_h' => '19:00 Evening',
	'stay_2_p1' => 'By 19:00 the lobby has thinned and the bar is the room that is still awake. Priya Raman runs it daily from 17:00 to 00:30: an oak counter, a few tables, and enough quiet that a conversation stays at the table where it started. Water is poured without a request. If the day was a meeting, the same tables work for a last look at notes before you close the laptop.',
	'stay_2_p2' => 'The evening does not come with a programme. You can eat elsewhere in Wrenfield and come back for a glass, or stay in and keep the chair. Staff will not move you on. When you are ready for the room, the desk is still there, and the key you already hold is the only thing you need.',
	'stay_3_h' => '23:00 Night',
	'stay_3_p1' => 'At 23:00 the house turns down. Quiet hours run from 23:00 to 07:00. Corridors stay dim, doors close softly, and the bar stops serving at 00:30. Aiyana Brooks is the night manager. She keeps the desk through the small hours, so a question about a train, a lost adapter, or an early car does not wait until morning.',
	'stay_3_p2' => 'In the room the blackout is there when you want it, and the bedside lamp is enough to read by. A glass of water is the last thing housekeeping sets out. Nothing else is scheduled. If you are still at the desk in the room, the building will not interrupt you. Night at Akari Hotel is mostly the absence of noise, which is the point of booking it.',
	'stay_4_h' => '07:00 Breakfast',
	'stay_4_p1' => 'Breakfast is served from 07:00 to 10:30, and on Saturday and Sunday until 11:00. It is included in every rate, from the Studio at €148 to the Loft at €238, and in the long-stay Studio rate of €132. Jonas Weber bakes the bread nearby and sets the table by the large window: coffee, fruit, and the morning’s bread, with enough time to sit before a first call.',
	'stay_4_p2' => 'You do not book a slot. Come when you wake, within the hours. The room can wait; check-out is by 11:00, unless you have added a late check-out until 15:00 for €30. After breakfast the day is yours again, or it is a walk to Wrenfield Central in six minutes if the train is the reason you are packed.',
	'stay_5_h' => 'House rules',
	'stay_5_p1' => 'Three rules keep the house easy to share. Quiet hours are 23:00 to 07:00, which covers the corridors, the bar once it has closed, and the rooms themselves. Smoking is not allowed anywhere in the building. Pets are not accepted, including stays of a single night. Each room is for a maximum of two guests.',
	'stay_5_p2' => 'If you need the reading room, a half day of four hours is €90 and is arranged with Daniel Okafor before you arrive. The card that confirms a booking is not a charge for the stay itself; free cancellation stands until forty-eight hours before arrival. After that, the card is used as agreed in the reply we send. Ask the desk if a rule is unclear. Aiyana can answer it at night as clearly as Tomás can in the afternoon.',
	'rooms_word' => 'Rooms',
	'rooms_intro' => 'Forty-two rooms occupy the old printing house: twenty-four Studios, twelve Corner rooms, and six Lofts. Every room is for two guests at most, with its own bath and a desk.',
	'rooms_cta' => 'See the rates',
	'rooms_1_h' => 'Studio',
	'rooms_1_p1' => 'The Studio is 19 square metres, with a queen bed and a compact desk set by the window. There are twenty-four of them. The rate is €148 per night, breakfast included. Stay five nights or more and the same room is €132 per night. One window gives the desk its light; in the evening a lamp is enough. The bath is private: a walk-in shower and a stone basin, with towels folded and left ready.',
	'rooms_1_p2' => 'Two guests is the maximum. The room is meant for sleeping and for a working day that fits on one table, not for a second sofa or a cot we do not keep. Hannah K. used a Studio for two nights around an audit. Rafael M. kept one for six nights on the long-stay rate. Both were here to work, and the desk is the piece of furniture that decides whether the room is the right one.',
	'rooms_2_h' => 'Corner',
	'rooms_2_p1' => 'The Corner room is 26 square metres. A king bed sits where two windows meet, and an armchair takes the angle between them. Twelve Corner rooms are in the house. The rate is €186 per night, with breakfast included. The desk is in the room, as it is in every room, placed so the windows are for the bed and the chair rather than for the screen. The bath matches the house: walk-in shower, stone basin, soft light.',
	'rooms_2_p2' => 'Two guests is the maximum. Two of the twelve Corner rooms are step-free from the street door to the room and into the bath. Ask for one of those two when you send dates; they are not held back for a later upgrade, and they go when they are asked for. If both are taken, we say so in the reply, within one working day, and offer another room only if you want it.',
	'rooms_3_h' => 'Loft',
	'rooms_3_p1' => 'The Loft is 34 square metres, with a high ceiling, a king bed on the main floor, and a desk on a mezzanine reached by a steel stair. Six Lofts are in the house. The rate is €238 per night, breakfast included. The mezzanine is a working shelf, not a second bedroom: the bed below is the only bed, and two guests remain the maximum. Light comes from the tall room rather than from a corner pair of windows.',
	'rooms_3_p2' => 'The bath is the same private arrangement as the other rooms, with a walk-in shower and a stone basin. Ines and Theo took a Loft for one Saturday night with nothing planned. The stair is the thing to know about before you book. If you would rather keep the desk on the same floor as the bed, the Studio or the Corner is the clearer choice, and we will say so.',
	'rooms_amen1' => 'Wi-Fi in every room',
	'rooms_amen2' => 'Breakfast in every rate, 07:00 to 10:30',
	'rooms_amen3' => 'A desk in every room',
	'rooms_amen4' => 'Private bath: walk-in shower and stone basin',
	'rooms_amen5' => 'Blackout at the window, a lamp at the bed',
	'rooms_amen6' => 'Water at the bar, 17:00 to 00:30',
	'rooms_amen7' => 'Front desk open 24 hours',
	'rooms_amen8' => 'Two guests at most in a room',
	'rooms_4_h' => 'In the room and the house',
	'rooms_4_p1' => 'Every rate includes breakfast, Wi-Fi, and water at the bar. Every room has a desk. Blackout covers the window when you want the room dark, and the bedside lamp stays. Housekeeping is led by Lucie Morel; rooms are reset while you are out, and a turn-down is there at night if you are staying in. The reading room, a meeting room for six, can be booked for a half day of four hours at €90.',
	'rooms_4_p2' => 'Printing for work stays is arranged with Daniel Okafor. The bar, under Priya Raman, runs from 17:00 to 00:30. The front desk is open twenty-four hours. Quiet hours are 23:00 to 07:00. Smoking is not allowed. Pets are not accepted. There is no pool and no extra room type beyond the forty-two: twenty-four Studios, twelve Corners, six Lofts. Late check-out until 15:00 is €30 per room, added only if you ask.',
	'rooms_5_h' => 'Step-free rooms',
	'rooms_5_p1' => 'Two Corner rooms are step-free. The path from the entrance at 18 Tessel Street to the room, and from the room into the bath, does not include a step. They are king-bed Corner rooms at the same rate, €186 per night with breakfast, and they count toward the twelve Corners, not as a separate category. Tell us in the enquiry that you need a step-free room, with your dates.',
	'rooms_5_p2' => 'We reply within one working day and confirm only if one of the two is free. The Studio and the Loft are not step-free; the Loft’s mezzanine stair rules it out, and we will not describe them as if they were. If you use a chair or simply prefer not to manage a threshold, name that in the first message. Tomás Ferreira’s team notes it before the card is taken, so the room you confirm is the room you can enter.',
	'rates_word' => 'Rates',
	'rates_intro' => 'Rates are in euro, breakfast included in every room price. The list below is the same six lines we send when we reply to an enquiry.',
	'rates_cta' => 'Enquire about dates',
	'rates_1_h' => 'The six rates',
	'rates_2_h' => 'What the rate includes',
	'rates_2_p1' => 'Breakfast is included in every room rate, including the long-stay Studio at €132. It is served from 07:00 to 10:30, and on Saturday and Sunday until 11:00. Wi-Fi is included in the room and in the reading room. Water at the bar is included while the bar is open, from 17:00 to 00:30. The desk in the room is included; you do not hire it. Towels, the walk-in shower, and a made bed are part of the room, looked after by Lucie Morel’s team.',
	'rates_2_p2' => 'The late check-out at €30 and the reading room at €90 are the two additions with their own prices. Drinks beyond water at the bar are not folded into the night rate. Printing for a work stay is arranged with Daniel Okafor and is not listed as a sixth extra on this page. If something you need is not in this list, ask in the enquiry and we will say whether we have it.',
	'rates_3_h' => 'Cancellation and the card',
	'rates_3_p1' => 'A stay is confirmed with a card. Cancellation is free until forty-eight hours before arrival. After that point the card is used as set out in the reply we send when we confirm availability and the rate. The enquiry itself does not take the card. First you send dates and the room type. Within one working day we reply with availability and the rate.',
	'rates_3_p2' => 'You then confirm with the card if you want the room held. You arrive from 15:00 on the first day. Check-out is by 11:00, unless the €30 late check-out until 15:00 has been added. We do not ask for the card to view a rate. The six prices above are the prices in the reply, matched to the room and the length of stay you asked for.',
	'rates_4_h' => 'Check-in, check-out, and city tax',
	'rates_4_p1' => 'Check-in is from 15:00. Check-out is by 11:00. The front desk is open twenty-four hours, so the key can be collected later than 15:00 if your train into Wrenfield Central is later. The station is a six-minute walk from 18 Tessel Street. A late check-out until 15:00 is €30 per room and must be agreed before the morning you need it; it is not assumed because the desk is open.',
	'rates_4_p2' => 'City tax is paid at the desk. The amount is not stated here because it is set outside the room rate and is added when you settle, not inside the €148, €186, €238, or €132. Ask the desk on arrival what is due for your nights and your party. Two guests is the maximum in each room. Aiyana Brooks can take the tax at night if you are leaving before breakfast; you do not have to wait for the morning shift.',
	'breakfast_bar_word' => 'Breakfast',
	'breakfast_bar_intro' => 'Breakfast is included with every room, from 07:00 to 10:30, and until 11:00 on Saturday and Sunday. The bar is open daily from 17:00 to 00:30.',
	'breakfast_bar_cta' => 'Rates and what they include',
	'cap_breakfast' => '( Breakfast )',
	'cap_bar' => '( Bar )',
	'breakfast_bar_1_h' => 'Breakfast hours',
	'breakfast_bar_1_p1' => 'Breakfast runs from 07:00 to 10:30 every morning, and on Saturday and Sunday until 11:00. It is included in every room rate: Studio €148, Corner €186, Loft €238, and the long-stay Studio at €132 a night for five nights or more. You do not reserve a table the night before. Come down when you are ready, inside those hours.',
	'breakfast_bar_1_p2' => 'The room is beside a large window, and the sitting is unhurried because the house has forty-two rooms, not a banquet floor. Check-out remains 11:00, including on weekend mornings when breakfast itself runs until 11:00. If you need the room until 15:00, that is the late check-out at €30, arranged separately. Jonas Weber is the breakfast chef. The bread is baked nearby and brought in for service. Coffee is poured at the table.',
	'breakfast_bar_2_h' => 'A morning plate',
	'breakfast_bar_2_p1' => 'The morning table is a short list, and nothing on it has a price of its own because breakfast is already in the rate. Bread from the nearby bake, toasted or left as it arrived. Butter. A little jam. Fruit, cut and set in a bowl rather than arranged. Yoghurt. Eggs, cooked to order in a plain pan, not from a display. Coffee, and tea if you would rather. Water on the table. Juice.',
	'breakfast_bar_2_p2' => 'That is the sample. It changes with what Jonas Weber has that morning, and it does not grow a specials board. If you do not eat eggs or you need the bread kept separate from something else, tell the desk the evening before or tell the person pouring coffee. We will not invent a menu we cannot cook. Guests who leave before 07:00 can ask at night, and Aiyana Brooks will note it for the kitchen.',
	'breakfast_bar_3_h' => 'The bar',
	'breakfast_bar_3_p1' => 'The bar is open daily from 17:00 to 00:30. Priya Raman leads it. The room is small: an oak counter, a few glasses, low light, and tables where two people can talk without raising their voice. Water is included with the stay for as long as the bar is open. Anything poured besides water is chosen at the counter and is not part of the room rate.',
	'breakfast_bar_3_p2' => 'There is no schedule of events, no music list, and no requirement to come down. The evening chapter of the house, around 19:00, is simply when the room is at its most used. After 23:00, quiet hours have already begun in the corridors, so voices stay low even though service continues until 00:30. If you want a table held, tell Tomás Ferreira during the day. At night, Aiyana knows which chairs are free.',
	'breakfast_bar_4_h' => 'Jonas Weber and Priya Raman',
	'breakfast_bar_4_p1' => 'Jonas Weber runs breakfast and Priya Raman runs the bar. They are not the same service stretched across the day. Jonas is in the kitchen for the morning hours, 07:00 to 10:30, and until 11:00 on Saturday and Sunday. His concern is the bread baked nearby, the eggs, the coffee, and a table by the window that is cleared properly between guests. Priya opens the bar at 17:00 and closes it at 00:30.',
	'breakfast_bar_4_p2' => 'Her concern is the counter, the pace of the room, and the fact that many guests are still halfway through a working day when they sit down. You can pass a whole stay and see only one of them. Mara Lindqvist, the general manager, keeps the two rooms on the same standard: calm, specific, and finished on time. Questions about a plate go to Jonas’s team in the morning. Questions about the evening go to Priya.',
	'work_stays_word' => 'Work',
	'work_stays_intro' => 'Work stays use the same rooms and the same desk that is already in each of them. Daniel Okafor handles these enquiries, including the long-stay Studio rate and the reading room.',
	'work_stays_cta' => 'Long-stay rate and reading room',
	'work_wide_label' => '( The desk )',
	'work_stays_1_h' => 'A desk in the room',
	'work_stays_1_p1' => 'Every room has a desk. In the Studio it sits by the window beside the queen bed, in 19 square metres. In the Corner room the king bed and two windows take the angle, and the desk stays on the main floor of the 26 square metres. In the Loft the desk is on the mezzanine, above the king bed, up the steel stair, in 34 square metres. The desk is for one person working, even when two guests share the room.',
	'work_stays_1_p2' => 'Two is the maximum. Wi-Fi is included. The chair is a working chair, and the lamp is there for after dusk. If you are in the house for an audit, a deadline, or a run of meetings, say so when you enquire. Daniel Okafor looks after guest relations for work stays. He reads that note before we reply, so the room we offer is the one whose desk matches the way you work.',
	'work_stays_2_h' => 'Long stay and the reading room',
	'work_stays_2_p1' => 'A Studio held for five nights or more is €132 per night, breakfast included, instead of €148. The long-stay rate does not apply to the Corner or the Loft. Rafael M. used it for six nights with a deadline in the room. A stay of four nights or fewer remains on the nightly rate of the room you choose. The reading room is the meeting room for six. A half day, four hours, is €90.',
	'work_stays_2_p2' => 'It has an oak table and linen chairs, and it is booked beside the bedroom, not instead of one. Printing is available for work stays; Daniel Okafor sets it up once the room is confirmed. Name the hours you want the reading room in the same message as your dates and room type. We reply within one working day with availability and both rates, the night and the half day, if you asked for both.',
	'work_stays_3_h' => 'Quiet hours and a working evening',
	'work_stays_3_p1' => 'Quiet hours run from 23:00 to 07:00. They suit a long task better than they suit a reception. If you still have a call at the desk in your room, keep it inside those hours’ expectation: low voice, door shut. The bar, open from 17:00 to 00:30 under Priya Raman, is the place for a conversation that should not happen in the corridor. Around 19:00 the house is in its evening chapter, and a table there can hold a notebook as easily as a glass.',
	'work_stays_3_p2' => 'Water is included. The front desk, led in the daytime by Tomás Ferreira and at night by Aiyana Brooks, is open twenty-four hours if a document must be collected or a car ordered. Smoking is not allowed. Pets are not accepted. The building is a 1931 printing house opened as Akari Hotel in 2019, and it still behaves like a place where people are trying to finish something.',
	'work_stays_4_h' => 'Arriving for work',
	'work_stays_4_p1' => 'Send the dates, the room type, and whether you need the reading room. Within one working day we reply with availability and the rate. You confirm with a card. Cancellation is free until forty-eight hours before arrival. Check-in is from 15:00, check-out by 11:00. Late check-out until 15:00 is €30 per room if the morning of departure is still a working morning.',
	'work_stays_4_p2' => 'Breakfast, included, is 07:00 to 10:30, weekend until 11:00, if you want it before you leave for a meeting. Wrenfield Central is a six-minute walk from 18 Tessel Street, which is usually the more reliable way to a first appointment than a car from the door. City tax is paid at the desk and is not inside the room rate. Daniel Okafor is the person named on work stays. If he is off the desk, the reply still comes within one working day.',
	'neighbourhood_word' => 'Wrenfield',
	'neighbourhood_intro' => 'The hotel is at 18 Tessel Street, Wrenfield, a six-minute walk from Wrenfield Central. Mornings point toward a bakery, a park, and the river; evenings can stay on the street or come back to the bar.',
	'neighbourhood_cta' => 'Back to the house',
	'nb_cap' => '( Tessel Street, 20:10 )',
	'nb_cap_p' => 'The street after dinner: brick, glass, and the entrance lit at the end of it.',
	'neighbourhood_1_h' => 'Wrenfield and the station',
	'neighbourhood_1_p1' => 'Akari Hotel stands at 18 Tessel Street, Wrenfield. Wrenfield Central station is a six-minute walk from the door. The street outside is quiet in the evening: brick and glass, warm windows, the occasional cyclist. The house itself was a printing works in 1931 and has been a hotel since 2019. You do not need a car to arrive or to leave. If your train is after check-in, the desk is open twenty-four hours and the room waits.',
	'neighbourhood_1_p2' => 'If you leave for an early train, check-out is still by 11:00 unless you have added the late check-out, but nothing stops you leaving earlier and walking the six minutes. Tomás Ferreira’s team will keep a bag for the afternoon if you have a meeting after you have given the room back. The neighbourhood is part of the stay only in the sense that the door opens onto a real street, not a forecourt.',
	'neighbourhood_2_h' => 'A morning walk',
	'neighbourhood_2_p1' => 'After breakfast, or before it if you are out and back by 10:30, the morning walk is three ordinary things in a row. A bakery on the corner, where the bread Jonas Weber serves has a relative, though breakfast at the hotel is already included and you need not buy another loaf. A park with a plain path and enough benches. A river, which in this part of Wrenfield reads more as a canal: a tree-lined path, people walking, no boat you are expected to board.',
	'neighbourhood_2_p2' => 'Nothing on this walk has a ticket we sell. The six-minute station walk is a different direction; do not treat the bakery, the park, and the river as if they were on the way to the platform. Go out, come back, and the desk will still be the desk. On Saturday and Sunday breakfast runs until 11:00, so a slightly longer loop still ends at a table.',
	'neighbourhood_3_h' => 'Three evenings',
	'neighbourhood_3_p1' => 'Three places are enough for a short stay, and none of them belongs to the hotel. The Lantern Room is a small dining room a short stroll from Tessel Street, the kind of place you book or you do not, depending on the night. Paper and Rye is a shop for books and a glass, open into the evening, where talking stays at the table. The Canal House shows one film at a time and sells nothing else worth planning around.',
	'neighbourhood_3_p2' => 'Hours change, so ask at our desk before you go; we will not pretend to publish their times. If none of them suits the night, Priya Raman’s bar is open from 17:00 to 00:30 and water is included with your room. The city is there. The hotel does not run a tour of it. Ines and Theo, who kept a Loft for a Saturday with no plans, used the evening this way: one place, then back.',
	'neighbourhood_4_h' => 'Coming back after dark',
	'neighbourhood_4_p1' => 'The walk from Wrenfield Central after dark is the same six minutes. The entrance is lit. If you are arriving for the first time, check-in is from 15:00 and the person with the key is at the desk. Aiyana Brooks is there through the night. Quiet hours inside the hotel start at 23:00; the street is simply a street, and we do not set rules for it.',
	'neighbourhood_4_p2' => 'The bar can still be open until 00:30 if you want a last half hour inside rather than another place in the neighbourhood. For a work stay, Daniel Okafor would rather you had the morning bakery and the station walk clear in your head than a long list. Tessel Street is the address to give a driver if you ever use one: 18 Tessel Street, Wrenfield. The phone, if the door is confusing at dusk, is +00 (0)20 4180 1931.',
	'people_word' => 'People',
	'people_intro' => 'Seven people run Akari Hotel day to day. You will meet some of them at the desk, at breakfast, or at the bar, and you can ask for any of them by name.',
	'people_cta' => 'Write to the house',
	'people_1_h' => 'Mara Lindqvist',
	'people_1_p1' => 'Mara Lindqvist is the general manager. She is responsible for the house as a whole: the forty-two rooms, the desk, the breakfast room, the bar, and the standard those rooms are held to. The building opened as Akari Hotel in 2019, in a printing house that dates from 1931, and Mara is who you are writing to if a stay has gone off the brief in a way a single department should not patch alone.',
	'people_1_p2' => 'She does not sit at the front desk for every arrival. Tomás Ferreira does that. Mara reads the exceptions, the step-free room requests, the long stays, and the tone of the replies that go out within one working day. Guests rarely need her name on the first night. It is on this page so you know who stands behind the other six. If you ask for her, the desk will say whether she is in the building.',
	'people_2_h' => 'Tomás Ferreira',
	'people_2_p1' => 'Tomás Ferreira leads the front office. Check-in from 15:00 is his room: the oak desk, the brass key, the note of your arrival time, the bag you leave if the room is not ready. He keeps the six-minute walk from Wrenfield Central in the directions he gives, and he is the person who confirms a late check-out at €30 until 15:00 when the morning needs it.',
	'people_2_p2' => 'Enquiries land with the house and are answered within one working day; Tomás’s team is who turns dates and a room type into a room number once you have confirmed with a card. During the day he knows which of the two step-free Corner rooms is free. He will not guess. If you are a work stay, he passes the desk and printing notes to Daniel Okafor rather than collecting them in a drawer of his own.',
	'people_3_h' => 'Aiyana Brooks',
	'people_3_p1' => 'Aiyana Brooks is the night manager. The front desk is open twenty-four hours, and the hours after the evening belongs to her. Quiet hours run from 23:00 to 07:00. She keeps the corridors calm and still answers the phone, +00 (0)20 4180 1931, if a train is late or a key misbehaves. The bar closes at 00:30; after that the desk is the only counter still staffed.',
	'people_3_p2' => 'A glass of water and a lamp by the bed are the night’s preparation, and Aiyana is who housekeeping has told if something in a room could not be finished. You can ask her about an early breakfast note for Jonas Weber, or about city tax if you are leaving before the morning team is back at full strength. She would rather solve the thing at 01:00 than leave it on a pad.',
	'people_4_h' => 'Jonas Weber',
	'people_4_p1' => 'Jonas Weber is the breakfast chef. Service is 07:00 to 10:30, and on Saturday and Sunday until 11:00. Breakfast is included in every rate, so his conversation with you is about the plate, not about a bill. The bread is baked nearby. Eggs are cooked to order. Fruit, yoghurt, coffee, tea, juice, and water make up the rest of the sample he is willing to put in writing.',
	'people_4_p2' => 'If you leave before 07:00, tell Aiyana Brooks at night and she will leave Jonas the note. He does not run the bar and he does not set room rates. On a work-stay morning he expects some guests to eat quickly and some to take the window table until the hour ends. Both are fine. The kitchen is sized for forty-two rooms, and the sitting stays inside that number.',
	'people_5_h' => 'Priya Raman',
	'people_5_p1' => 'Priya Raman leads the bar. It opens at 17:00 and closes at 00:30 every day. The room is an oak counter, low light, and a few tables. Water for guests is included; other drinks are not part of the room rate. Around 19:00 the bar is the evening chapter of the house, a place to sit after you have come back from Tessel Street or from one of the three neighbourhood rooms we are willing to name.',
	'people_5_p2' => 'Priya keeps the volume fit for quiet hours once 23:00 has passed, even though last service is 00:30. You can work at a table if the work is quiet. She will not turn the bar into a meeting room; that is the reading room, at €90 for four hours, and Daniel Okafor books it. If you want a chair held, the front desk will tell her.',
	'people_6_h' => 'Lucie Morel',
	'people_6_p1' => 'Lucie Morel leads housekeeping. Her team turns the forty-two rooms: queen beds in the Studios, king beds in the Corner rooms and Lofts, desks cleared only of what is clearly finished, baths reset with the walk-in shower and the stone basin left as they should be. A turn-down at night is a lamp, an open book if you left one, a glass of water.',
	'people_6_p2' => 'During the day, if you are still in the room, they pass and come back. Bags left at the desk before 15:00 go up when she says the room is ready, which is the message Tomás relays. Lucie does not take bookings. If something in the room is wrong, tell the desk and it reaches her the same day, including on Aiyana’s shift. Two of the Corner rooms are step-free; her team knows which doors those are.',
	'people_7_h' => 'Daniel Okafor',
	'people_7_p1' => 'Daniel Okafor looks after guest relations for work stays. He is the person to name if you need the long-stay Studio rate of €132 a night for five nights or more, the reading room at €90 for a half day of four hours, or printing while you are here. Every room already has a desk; Daniel’s job is to match the desk to the stay, not to invent a business floor the house does not have.',
	'people_7_p2' => 'A Studio is 19 square metres with the desk by the window. A Loft puts the desk on the mezzanine. He will say which is sensible if you ask. He works with Tomás on the reply, which still comes within one working day, and with Mara Lindqvist when a stay needs a decision above a room assignment. The card confirmation and the forty-eight-hour free cancellation are the same as for any other guest.',
	'faq_word' => 'Questions',
	'faq_intro' => 'Ten answers cover arrival, the rooms, and payment. The times, prices, and limits are the same ones used at the desk.',
	'faq_g1' => 'Arrival',
	'faq_1_q' => 'What time can I check in and when do I leave?',
	'faq_1_a' => 'Check-in is from 15:00. Check-out is by 11:00. The front desk is open twenty-four hours, so you may arrive later than 15:00 on the day your room is held and still collect the key from Tomás Ferreira’s team, or from Aiyana Brooks if it is late. A room is not ready before 15:00. If you are early, you may leave a suitcase and use the lobby. Late check-out until 15:00 is €30 per room and only applies if it has been added to the booking. It is not included in the Studio, Corner, Loft, or long-stay rate. On Saturday and Sunday, breakfast runs until 11:00, which meets check-out but does not extend it. The six-minute walk to Wrenfield Central is the same in the afternoon and in the morning. Put the arrival time in your enquiry if it is much later than 15:00, so the desk expects you.',
	'faq_2_q' => 'How do I reach 18 Tessel Street?',
	'faq_2_a' => 'The address is 18 Tessel Street, Wrenfield. From Wrenfield Central station the walk takes six minutes. The entrance is an oak and glass door, lit at dusk, on a quiet street of brick and glass. If you are being driven, give that address; there is no separate coach entrance. Check-in starts at 15:00, and the person who meets you is at the front desk inside the lobby. The telephone, if the door is hard to spot, is +00 (0)20 4180 1931. The email is hello@akari-hotel.example. We reply to enquiries within one working day. The building was a printing house in 1931 and has been Akari Hotel since 2019. You do not need a code for the street door during desk hours, and the desk never closes. Aiyana Brooks covers the night walk-up from the station after the bar has closed at 00:30.',
	'faq_3_q' => 'Is anyone at the desk at night?',
	'faq_3_a' => 'Yes. The front desk is staffed twenty-four hours. Aiyana Brooks is the night manager. Quiet hours in the house run from 23:00 to 07:00, which changes how the corridors sound, not whether you can reach a person. The bar, led by Priya Raman, closes at 00:30. After that, questions come to the desk: a key, a taxi, an early note for breakfast, city tax if you are leaving before the morning team. The phone +00 (0)20 4180 1931 is answered. Breakfast itself does not start until 07:00, or you may sleep through it. If you will arrive after 23:00, say so when you confirm. The card you gave when you confirmed still holds the room, and check-in remains the same arrival, only later. Nothing in the night shift replaces a booking. You still need a confirmed room before you travel.',
	'faq_4_q' => 'Can I leave my bag if the room is not ready?',
	'faq_4_a' => 'Yes. Before 15:00, or on the morning of departure after you have checked out by 11:00, the desk will take a suitcase. Lucie Morel’s housekeeping team releases the room; Tomás Ferreira’s team tells you when the key is ready and has the bag sent up. You can wait in the lobby. There is no separate luggage fee in our rates. The six published prices do not include a charge for holding a bag. If you have a meeting between giving the room back and your train, the six-minute walk to Wrenfield Central is easier without the case, and the desk expects that. Tell them if the bag should not go up without you. Pets are not accepted, and a bag is not a way around that rule. Smoking is not allowed in the lobby while you wait.',
	'faq_g2' => 'Rooms',
	'faq_5_q' => 'Which rooms can I book, and what is in them?',
	'faq_5_a' => 'There are forty-two rooms. Twenty-four are Studios: 19 square metres, queen bed, desk by the window, €148 per night. Twelve are Corner rooms: 26 square metres, king bed, two windows, €186 per night. Six are Lofts: 34 square metres, king bed, desk on a mezzanine with a steel stair, €238 per night. Each room takes two guests at most and has its own bath, with a walk-in shower and a stone basin. Breakfast is included. Wi-Fi is included. A Studio of five nights or more is €132 per night instead of €148. The reading room is not a bedroom; it is a meeting room for six at €90 for four hours. When you enquire, name the room type. We reply within one working day with availability and the rate that matches the length of stay. We will not switch you into a Loft unless you asked for one.',
	'faq_6_q' => 'Do you allow more than two guests, children, or pets?',
	'faq_6_a' => 'Each room takes a maximum of two guests. That applies to the Studio, the Corner, and the Loft. The mezzanine in the Loft is a desk, not a third bed. We do not add a cot and we do not join two rates into one room. If three people are travelling, book a second room. Children count toward the two. Pets are not accepted, for a night or for a long stay. Smoking is not allowed in the rooms or elsewhere in the building. Quiet hours from 23:00 to 07:00 apply to every guest. The reading room holds a meeting of up to six during a booked half day, and those six are not overnight guests of that room. If your party does not fit these limits, say so in the enquiry anyway. We would rather reply that we cannot take the booking than discover it at 15:00 at the desk.',
	'faq_7_q' => 'Which rooms are step-free?',
	'faq_7_a' => 'Two Corner rooms are step-free from the entrance to the room and into the bath. They are among the twelve Corner rooms, at the same rate of €186 per night, breakfast included, king bed, 26 square metres. They are not a separate price and not an upgrade offered on the day. Ask for a step-free Corner when you send your dates. If one of the two is free, the reply within one working day will say so. If both are taken, we will say that too and will not move you into a Studio or a Loft while calling it step-free. The Loft has a steel stair to the desk mezzanine. The Studio is not one of the two step-free rooms. Lucie Morel’s team and Tomás Ferreira’s team both know which doors qualify. Repeat the request when you confirm with the card, so it stays on the booking.',
	'faq_g3' => 'Payment',
	'faq_8_q' => 'What does the nightly rate include?',
	'faq_8_a' => 'The nightly rate includes the room and breakfast. Breakfast is 07:00 to 10:30, and until 11:00 on Saturday and Sunday. Wi-Fi is included. Water at the bar is included from 17:00 to 00:30. The desk in the room is included. The Studio is €148, the Corner €186, the Loft €238, and a Studio of five nights or more is €132. Those figures are per night, in euro. Late check-out until 15:00 is not included; it is €30 per room. The reading room is not included; a half day of four hours is €90. Drinks other than water at the bar are not included. Printing for a work stay is arranged with Daniel Okafor and is not inside the night rate. City tax is not inside the night rate either. It is paid at the desk. The amount is not listed on this page.',
	'faq_9_q' => 'How do I confirm, and can I cancel?',
	'faq_9_a' => 'Send your dates and the room type. We reply within one working day with availability and the rate. You confirm with a card. Cancellation is free until forty-eight hours before arrival. After that, the card is used as stated in the confirmation. The enquiry email is hello@akari-hotel.example, and the phone is +00 (0)20 4180 1931. You do not confirm by walking in, though the desk can take a question at any hour. A reply that offers a rate is not yet a booking. The booking exists when you have confirmed and we have acknowledged the card. If you change the dates, send another message; a change follows the same forty-eight-hour rule relative to the arrival you are moving. The long-stay rate of €132 applies only when the Studio stay is five nights or more at the time it is confirmed.',
	'faq_10_q' => 'When do I pay the city tax?',
	'faq_10_a' => 'City tax is paid at the desk. It is not included in the €148, €186, €238, or €132 nightly rates, and it is not the €30 late check-out or the €90 reading room. The amount is not stated on the website. The desk tells you what is due for your nights when you settle. You can pay it on arrival from 15:00 or when you check out by 11:00. Aiyana Brooks can take it at night if that is when you are leaving. The card you gave to confirm the booking is for the reservation. Ask the desk whether the tax sits on that card or is taken separately; they will follow the rule in force for your dates and will say so before anything is taken. Two guests is the maximum the room was sold for, and the tax conversation uses the party you actually have in the room.',
	'enquire_word' => 'Enquire',
	'enquire_intro' => 'Send dates and a room type. We reply within one working day with availability and the rate, then hold the room once you confirm with a card.',
	'enquire_cta' => 'See the rates',
	'form_h' => 'Send an enquiry',
	'form_name' => 'Your name',
	'form_email' => 'Email',
	'form_phone' => 'Phone (optional)',
	'form_in' => 'Arrival',
	'form_out' => 'Departure',
	'form_room' => 'Room type',
	'form_studio' => 'Studio',
	'form_corner' => 'Corner',
	'form_loft' => 'Loft',
	'form_reading' => 'Add the reading room',
	'form_step' => 'I need a step-free Corner room',
	'form_msg' => 'Anything we should know',
	'form_send' => 'Send enquiry',
	'form_note' => 'We reply within one working day. No card is needed to send this.',
	'enquire_1_h' => 'Four steps',
	'enquire_1_s1' => 'send your dates and the room type you want: Studio, Corner, or Loft. Add the reading room if you need it, and say if you need one of the two step-free Corner rooms.',
	'enquire_1_s2' => 'we reply within one working day with availability and the rate. The rate will be one of the published figures: €148, €186, €238, or €132 a night for a Studio of five nights or more, plus €30 if you asked for late check-out until 15:00, plus €90 if you asked for the reading room for four hours.',
	'enquire_1_s3' => 'confirm with a card. Cancellation is free until forty-eight hours before arrival.',
	'enquire_1_s4' => 'arrive from 15:00 on the first day. Check-out is by 11:00. The front desk is open twenty-four hours.',
	'enquire_1_tail' => 'That is the whole path. A message that only asks a question is still welcome; you do not have to begin a booking to write.',
	'enquire_2_h' => 'Write to the house',
	'enquire_2_p1' => 'Use the form on this page, or write to hello@akari-hotel.example, or call +00 (0)20 4180 1931. Say the arrival date, the departure date, the room type, and the name the room should be held in. Add a telephone number if you want the reply by phone. Daniel Okafor reads the notes that mention work, printing, the long-stay rate, or the reading room. Tomás Ferreira’s team reads arrivals.',
	'enquire_2_p2' => 'We answer within one working day. The answer states whether the room is free and which rate applies. It does not ask you to create an account. Prices are in euro. We do not convert them in the reply. If you are writing for someone else, say so, and give the name of the guest who will check in. The card comes only at the confirmation step, not in the first message.',
	'enquire_3_h' => 'Address, station, and hours',
	'enquire_3_p1' => 'Akari Hotel, 18 Tessel Street, Wrenfield. The walk from Wrenfield Central station is six minutes. Check-in is from 15:00 and check-out is by 11:00. The front desk is open twenty-four hours, with Tomás Ferreira leading it by day and Aiyana Brooks at night. Breakfast is 07:00 to 10:30, and until 11:00 on Saturday and Sunday. The bar is 17:00 to 00:30. Quiet hours are 23:00 to 07:00.',
	'enquire_3_p2' => 'The building opened in 2019 in a printing house from 1931. Forty-two rooms: twenty-four Studios, twelve Corners, six Lofts. If you are already in Wrenfield and would rather ask at the desk than write, come to the lobby. An enquiry made in person follows the same four steps, and the reply to a request we cannot answer on the spot still comes within one working day. The entrance is on Tessel Street itself.',
	'enquire_4_h' => 'What to include if the stay is for work',
	'enquire_4_p1' => 'Name the deadline only if it changes the room you need. Otherwise the useful lines are: how many nights, whether the stay is five or more so the Studio can be rated at €132, whether the desk on a mezzanine is acceptable, and whether you need the reading room for six at €90 for four hours. Printing is arranged once the room is confirmed; mention it so Daniel Okafor sees it in the first reply.',
	'enquire_4_p2' => 'Two guests remain the maximum in the bedroom. The people in the reading room during the day are not extra overnight guests. Breakfast is included and can be taken before you leave for a meeting, from 07:00. The station walk is six minutes, which is often the simplest way to be on time. Mara Lindqvist does not need to be named in a routine work enquiry. Daniel is the right name.',
	'privacy_word' => 'Privacy',
	'privacy_intro' => 'This page says what Akari Hotel does with the details you send when you enquire or stay. Questions go to hello@akari-hotel.example or to the desk.',
	'privacy_1_h' => 'Who we are',
	'privacy_1_p1' => 'Akari Hotel is the hotel at 18 Tessel Street, Wrenfield. For a question about your stay or about the details you have sent us, write to hello@akari-hotel.example or call +00 (0)20 4180 1931. The front desk is open twenty-four hours. Mara Lindqvist is the general manager. This page describes what the hotel does with the details you give when you enquire, when you confirm, and when you stay.',
	'privacy_1_p2' => 'It does not describe any other business. We do not sell those details. We do not pass them to a list. Prices, room types, and hours on the website are published so you can enquire without handing over anything you do not wish to. You may also ask at the desk in person. A conversation at the desk is noted only as far as the booking itself requires.',
	'privacy_2_h' => 'What you send us',
	'privacy_2_p1' => 'An enquiry asks for dates, a room type, and a way to reply: your name, an email address, and a phone number if you offer one. If you mention a step-free Corner room, a long stay, the reading room, or printing, that note is part of the enquiry too. Daniel Okafor reads work-stay notes. Tomás Ferreira’s team reads the arrival. When you confirm, you give a card so the room can be held.',
	'privacy_2_p2' => 'Cancellation is free until forty-eight hours before arrival, and the card is used after that only as the confirmation described. We do not ask for a card in the first message. At the desk we may record the names of the guests in the room, up to the maximum of two, and what is due, including city tax, which is paid at the desk and is not part of the nightly rate. We do not ask you to create an account on the website.',
	'privacy_3_h' => 'Why we keep it',
	'privacy_3_p1' => 'We use the enquiry to reply within one working day with availability and the rate. We use the confirmation to hold the room you asked for, to prepare it, and to know whether you need one of the two step-free Corner rooms. We use a night note if you leave one for Aiyana Brooks, for example an early breakfast or a late arrival after 15:00.',
	'privacy_3_p2' => 'Housekeeping, led by Lucie Morel, sees the room and the timing, not a copy of your correspondence. The bar and the breakfast room do not keep a profile of what you drank or ate. Water at the bar is included; there is no tab to build a record from unless you order something further and settle it. We keep what the stay and the enquiry require, and we do not keep it for a purpose we have not named here. You may ask what we still hold.',
	'privacy_4_h' => 'Asking, correcting, and the desk',
	'privacy_4_p1' => 'Write to hello@akari-hotel.example if you want a copy of what we hold about an enquiry or a stay, if a name is wrong, or if you want a note deleted when we no longer need it to complete the booking or to meet the cancellation terms. Call +00 (0)20 4180 1931 if that is easier, day or night. We will say if something must be kept until the forty-eight hours before arrival have passed and the stay is settled, including city tax at the desk.',
	'privacy_4_p2' => 'We will not ask you to accept a new use of your details as a condition of a rate. The rates remain the published ones: €148, €186, €238, €132, €30, and €90. Staff named on the site use guest details only to do the work of their roles. If a message reaches the wrong person inside the house, it is still not sent outside it.',
	'journal_word' => 'Journal',
	'journal_intro' => 'Guest notes from the house. Each one names the room and the number of nights, and none of them is a rating.',
	'nf_word' => 'Lost',
	'nf_intro' => 'This page is not on any floor of the house. The front desk is open all day and night, and the rooms, rates and the enquiry form are one click away.',
	'nf_cta' => 'Back to the front desk',
);
